---
import ContentLayout from "../layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import { IS_TWITCH_ONLY } from "../lib/constants";

const mainUrl = Astro.site?.toString() ?? "https://mixchat.grepfs.xyz/";
const title = "Privacy Policy - Mixchat";
const description =
    "Mixchat Privacy Policy: no server-side database, no message storage, OAuth tokens stored in secure httpOnly cookies only.";
---

<ContentLayout {title} {description} ogUrl={`${mainUrl}privacy`}>
    <Fragment slot="head">
        <style>
            body {
                font-family:
                    -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, "Segoe UI", Roboto,
                    Oxygen, Ubuntu, Cantarell, sans-serif;
                line-height: 1.6;
                max-width: 900px;
                margin: 0 auto;
                padding: 20px;
                background: #0a0a0a;
                color: #e0e0e0;
            }
            h1 {
                color: #fff;
                margin-bottom: 10px;
            }
            h2 {
                color: var(--color-accent);
                margin-top: 30px;
            }
            .meta {
                color: #999;
                font-size: 14px;
                margin-bottom: 30px;
            }
            a {
                color: var(--color-accent);
                text-decoration: none;
            }
            a:hover {
                text-decoration: underline;
            }
            code {
                background: #1a1a1a;
                padding: 2px 6px;
                border-radius: 3px;
                font-family: "Courier New", monospace;
            }
        </style>
    </Fragment>
    <div style="text-align: center; margin-bottom: 30px;">
        <img
            src={IS_TWITCH_ONLY ? "/logo-twitch.webp" : "/logo.webp"}
            alt="Mixchat Logo"
            width="80"
            height="80"
            style="width: auto; height: 80px;"
        />
    </div>
    <h1>Privacy Policy</h1>
    <p class="meta">Last updated: March 29, 2026</p>

    <h2>1. Overview</h2>
    <p>
        Mixchat is designed to function without a server-side database for chat
        data. No messages, emails, or account credentials are stored on any
        server. Data processing occurs locally in the visitor's browser. Once
        the user logs out or clears their cache, session data is removed.
    </p>
    <p>
        The one exception is the optional <strong>Song Queue</strong> streamer
        tool, which creates a small server-side session record so the streamer
        can share a read-only listening page with chatters. See section 3.1
        below for the full details.
    </p>

    <h2>2. Data Collection</h2>

    <h3>
        2.1 Logging In{IS_TWITCH_ONLY ? " (Twitch)" : " (Twitch & YouTube)"}
    </h3>
    <p>
        When connecting a Twitch{IS_TWITCH_ONLY ? "" : " or YouTube"} account,
        the browser gets "keys" (OAuth tokens) from{" "}
        {IS_TWITCH_ONLY ? "the service" : "those services"}.
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>Secure Storage:</strong> Key tokens are stored as{" "}
            <code>httpOnly</code> cookies. This helps block outside scripts from
            seeing them.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>UI Session:</strong> A little bit of info (like your
            username and profile picture) is saved in <code>localStorage</code>{" "}
            so the app remembers who you are on refresh.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <h3>2.2 Your Messages</h3>
    <p>
        Mixchat is a real-time client. Chat messages flow from Twitch
        {IS_TWITCH_ONLY ? "" : " or YouTube"}
        directly to the screen. They are kept in the browser's memory during a
        session and are not archived.
    </p>

    {!IS_TWITCH_ONLY && (
        <>
            <h3>2.3 YouTube Integration</h3>
            <p>
                By linking a YouTube channel, Mixchat uses the official YouTube
                Data API. It only requests public info to show live status and
                stream metadata properly.
            </p>
        </>
    )}

    <h2>3. Third-Party Services</h2>
    <p>
        To make everything work smoothly, Mixchat talks to a few core services:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>Twitch{IS_TWITCH_ONLY ? "" : " & YouTube"}:</strong> For the
            chat and video feeds.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Emote Providers (7TV, BTTV, FFZ):</strong> To render those
            custom emotes everyone loves. These services might see which channel
            you're watching so they can serve the right emote pack.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>3.1 Song Queue Sessions</h2>
    <p>
        When a streamer enables the Song Queue tool inside Stream tools,
        Mixchat creates a small server-side session record (a JSON file) that
        contains:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>The session title and a unique 24-character session id.</li>
        <li>
            The list of queued YouTube videos, including the submitter's display
            name (Twitch username or YouTube channel name) and avatar URL.
        </li>
        <li>
            A SHA-256 hash of the streamer's owner token. The owner token itself
            is returned to the streamer once and never stored on our servers.
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        If the streamer toggles <strong>Public link</strong> on, anyone with the
        link can read a stripped-down view of the queue (video ids, titles,
        submitter display names) at <code>/s/&lt;id&gt;</code>. Public viewers
        cannot see submitter user ids or the streamer's account.
    </p>
    <p>
        No OAuth tokens, email addresses, or passwords are stored in the song
        queue record. Ending the queue deletes it immediately.
    </p>

    <h2>4. Stateless and Database-Free (with One Exception)</h2>
    <p>
        Mixchat's server/API is "stateless" for chat and OAuth data. This means
        it doesn't remember sessions from one second to the next without the
        browser sending its security key. There is no backend database for
        messages, accounts, or emote data.
    </p>
    <p>
        The Song Queue feature (see section 3.1) is the one piece of stateful
        server-side storage. It is opt-in and scoped to the streamer's own
        channel.
    </p>

    <h2>5. Data Retention and Deletion</h2>
    <p>
        Since no data is stored, "deleting an account" is straightforward.
        Clicking <strong>Logout</strong> wipes the cookies and session info from
        the browser. Closing the tab clears the memory usage. For total
        assurance, clear the site data in the browser settings.
    </p>

    <h2>6. Security</h2>
    <p>
        Login tokens are managed using secure HTTP-only cookies. It is
        recommended to use strong passwords and two-factor authentication (2FA)
        on Twitch{IS_TWITCH_ONLY ? "" : " and YouTube"} accounts.
    </p>

    <h2>7. Changes</h2>
    <p>
        Mixchat changes fast. The date at the top will be updated if there are
        ever changes to how info is handled (like adding a new feature that
        needs a different API).
    </p>

    <h2>8. Contact</h2>
    <p>
        If you have questions about any of this, open an issue on the
        <a href="https://forgejo.grepfs.xyz/grepfs17/Mixchat" target="_blank">
            {" "}
            Forgejo repository
        </a>
        .
    </p>

    <hr style="margin-top: 50px; border: none; border-top: 1px solid #333;" />
    <p style="text-align: center; color: #666; font-size: 12px; margin-top: 20px;">
        <a href="/">Back to Mixchat</a>
    </p>
</ContentLayout>
